00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.380
Okay, let's pop the hood on pure quantization.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:36.460
Everyone talks about quantization as a simple

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:37.880
make it smaller knob.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.100
In practice, it is a set of deliberate trade-offs

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.420
between bit width, calibration data, and hardware mapping

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:46.960
that determine whether a model stays usable.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:51.120
Pure quantization methods skip iMATrix calibration

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.260
and instead apply uniform or near uniform scaling across weights,

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:57.900
which reduces pre-processing time

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:01.500
and sometimes improves throughput on CPUs during offload.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:04.780
The cost appears in slightly higher perplexity,

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:10.100
typically 0.05 to 0.17 above eMATrix equivalents

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:11.460
on the same model size.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:15.000
For MOE architectures, the savings compound

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.240
because only active experts are loaded.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:22.380
Dropping from 4.5 bits per weight to 4.22 bits per weight

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.780
can free enough VRAM to keep an extra expert resident

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:27.560
and raise cache hit rates.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:30.380
The practical decision rule is straightforward.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:33.120
Use pure quants when generation speed

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:35.540
on partial offload matters more than squeezing

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:37.800
every last point of benchmark quality.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:41.480
And fall back to iMATrix when you have the calibration budget

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:44.760
and need maximum fidelity on long-context tasks.

